166	379	page	outreach	{"slug":"outreach","status":"published","title":"OUTREACH","content":"<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">\\n<div class=\\"image-border-blue\\"><img style=\\"max-width: 75%;\\" src=\\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49607319987_ca4b19f54d_3k.jpg\\"></div>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">Our Outreach Work</div>\\n<p>The LigerBots pride themselves on our strong relationships in our community, and the work we do to motivate others to get involved in STEM. Click on the links below to explore some of our interactions with the community!</p>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">AMP</div>\\n<p>Click <a href=\\"/amp\\">here</a> to learn more about the Awesome Mentorship Program.</p>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">FLL</div>\\n<p>Click <a href=\\"/fll\\">here</a> to learn more about our work with FLL.</p>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">Educational Resources</div>\\n<p>Click <a href=\\"/educational-resources\\">here</a> to access our STEAM Activity Flyers and our Whitepapers.</p>"}	{"slug":"outreach","status":"published","title":"OUTREACH","content":"<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">\\n<div class=\\"image-border-blue\\"><img style=\\"max-width: 75%;\\" src=\\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49607319987_ca4b19f54d_3k.jpg\\"></div>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">Our Outreach Work</div>\\n<p>The LigerBots pride themselves on our strong relationships in our community, and the work we do to motivate others to get involved in STEM. Click on the links below to explore some of our interactions with the community!</p>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">AMP</div>\\n<p>Click <a href=\\"/amp\\">here</a> to learn more about the Awesome Mentorship Program.</p>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">FLL</div>\\n<p>Click <a href=\\"/fll\\">here</a> to learn more about our work with FLL.</p>\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">Educational Resources</div>\\n<p>Click <a href=\\"/educational-resources\\">here</a> to access our STEAM Activity Flyers and our Whitepapers.</p>"}	\N	\N
167	385	page	fll	{"slug":"fll","title":"FLL","content":"<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">\\n<figure><img style=\\"max-width: 100%;\\" src=\\"https://farm5.staticflickr.com/4541/37808387264_34b49ca6ca_k.jpg\\"></figure>\\n<div class=\\"image-border-blue\\">&nbsp;</div>\\n</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">FIRST<small>&reg;</small> LEGO<small>&reg;</small> League (FLL) Newton Qualifier and<br>Massachusetts East Championship</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The LigerBots host the FLL Newton Qualifier every year at Newton North High School on the last Saturday before Thanksgiving, and often host the Massachusetts East FLL Championship at the high school in mid-December.<a href=\\"https://flic.kr/s/aHBqjB3Rj6\\">The last Newton Qualifier</a> was November 18, 2023, and the <a href=\\"https://flic.kr/s/aHBqjB7CE7\\">Massachusetts East FLL Championship</a> was December 16, 2023.</p>\\n<p>At the these FLL events, teams of students in grades 4 to 8 compete using LEGO robots they design, build, and program to perform complex tasks in a novel challenge created by FIRST every year. The 2023 challenge was called <a href=\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zI_vz9YUw1g\\">Masterpiece</a>, which had an arts theme. (A unified theme carries through each year in all of FIRST's programs, from K-12.) FLL teams also compete by giving presentations they have researched and created on a topic related to the game theme. You can learn more about the 2023 season on the <a href=\\"https://www.first-lego-league.org/en/2023-24-season/the-masterpiece-season\\">FIRST website.</a> The FIRST 2024/25 game is called <a href=\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4JV7x014U0I\\">Submerged</a>, with an ocean theme. FIRST will launch the 2024/25 season on August 6, 2024.</p>\\n<p>The LigerBots host a STEAM expo at the MA East FLL Championship, with hands-on activities for kids in the fields of science, technology, engineering, art, and math, presented by team members and by local organizations. The public is always invited to watch our tournaments and to enjoy the activities at the STEAM expo. More than 1000 people usually attend our two FLL events, including FLL team members, families, LigerBots volunteers, and members of the public.&nbsp;</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>Our STEAM expo at the 2023 MA East FLL Championship included 11 activities provided by local companies and organizations, plus 15 more activities and six robot demonstrations offered by the LigerBots and other FIRST Robotics Competition (FRC) and FIRST Tech Challenge (FTC) teams. If your organization would like to bring a hands-on activity for kids to our next STEAM expo, please email <strong><a href=\\"mailto:fll@ligerbots.org\\">fll@ligerbots.org</a></strong>.</p>\\n<p>You can find hundreds of photos from the 2023 events on the <a href=\\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/ligerbots/albums/\\">LigerBots Flickr</a> page.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">FLL Information Night</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The LigerBots will host its annual FIRST LEGO League (FLL) information night on Saturday, June 22, 2024, from 3:00 to 4:30 p.m., at the<a href=\\"https://www.wpsinstitute.org/wps-cll\\"> WPS Community Learning Lab,</a> 160 Herrick Road, Newton, MA 02459. If you would like to attend &mdash; or cannot attend but are interested &mdash; please fill out <a href=\\"https://forms.gle/sZV4F4t8m43R1Fti8\\">this form.</a></p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>If you cannot attend, a link to the presentation will be posted on this page after the event.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<p>For more background on the FLL program, here is the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll\\">official FLL website</a> and <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/about/vision-and-mission\\">FIRST&rsquo;s mission statement</a>, this year&rsquo;s <a href=\\"https://info.firstinspires.org/first-dive\\">FLL challenge and season info,</a> and the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll/calendar\\">FLL season calendar</a>. You can also email <strong><a href=\\"mailto:fll@ligerbots.org\\">fll@ligerbots.org</a></strong> for more information.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">About FIRST<small>&reg;</small> LEGO<small>&reg;</small> League</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>FLL immerses students in grades pre-K to 8 in real-world scientific and technological problems, such as food safety or recycling. There are three levels of FLL based on the students&rsquo; grade: FLL Discover, FLL Explore, and FLL Challenge. FLL Discover is a playful introduction to STEM for children in grades pre-K to 1. FLL Explore, for students in grades 2 to 4, helps students explore while they learn about the design and coding of FLL robots by using powered LEGO&reg; bricks to create solutions to problems. FLL Challenge is a competition for students in grades 4 to 8 that teaches them to build and program autonomous LEGO&reg; robots that can compete on a 4&rsquo; x 8&rsquo; field in the annual robot game designed by the FIRST organization, performing assigned missions such as moving a ball or flipping a switch. FLL Challenge teams also research a real-world problem within guidelines provided by FIRST and design solutions that can be presented to judges at an FLL tournament.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>In addition to helping to teach students about STEAM through hands-on activities, experience in FLL is good preparation for the high-school-level FIRST Robotics Competition (FRC) program, whether with the LigerBots or any other FRC team. The LigerBots are committed to doing what the team can to support FLL programs, including acting as an information clearinghouse and supporting FLL coaches who are figuring out how to get an FLL team up and running.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>New teams are typically formed by two to three parents interested in providing an FLL experience for their kids. Teams typically meet once or twice a week for two to three hours, and the season is approximately 10 weeks long. Parent coaches do not have to be engineers or scientists! The challenge is designed to be solved by kids. Teams can have up to 10 student members. While teams tend to come and go as children enter and then age out of the FLL program, there are a few legacy teams that occasionally have openings for new team members.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<p>The first requirement for forming a team is to have space for a 4' x 8' game table. It costs about $1100 in materials to start an FLL team and about $500 in recurring costs each year. Startup costs include a robot controller module kit (<a href=\\"https://www.lego.com/en-us/categories/robots-for-kids\\">Spike</a> or EV3), which is about $400, a set of specialized LEGOs called Technics, that costs about another $500, and materials to build a table, which cost about $150. Recurring expenses include a $250 team registration fee that goes to FIRST, a &ldquo;challenge set&rdquo; sold by FIRST for about $100 that includes a game mat and LEGOs specific to that year&rsquo;s challenge, and a registration fee of about $100 per competition for each level the team makes it to. The cost can be divided by the number of participants, or the team can apply for grants. Of course, if teams want to spend more money, they can, but they do not need to. Here is the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll/challenge/pricing-and-payment\\">FLL pricing and payment payment page</a> on the FIRST website.</p>\\n<p>&nbsp;For more information on how to start a team, you can visit the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll/start-a-team\\">FIRST&reg; website</a>, or you can email the LigerBots at fll@ligerbots.org.</p>","status":"published"}	{"slug":"fll","title":"FLL","content":"<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">\\n<figure><img style=\\"max-width: 100%;\\" src=\\"https://farm5.staticflickr.com/4541/37808387264_34b49ca6ca_k.jpg\\"></figure>\\n<div class=\\"image-border-blue\\">&nbsp;</div>\\n</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">FIRST<small>&reg;</small> LEGO<small>&reg;</small> League (FLL) Newton Qualifier and<br>Massachusetts East Championship</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The LigerBots host the FLL Newton Qualifier every year at Newton North High School on the last Saturday before Thanksgiving, and often host the Massachusetts East FLL Championship at the high school in mid-December.<a href=\\"https://flic.kr/s/aHBqjB3Rj6\\">The last Newton Qualifier</a> was November 18, 2023, and the <a href=\\"https://flic.kr/s/aHBqjB7CE7\\">Massachusetts East FLL Championship</a> was December 16, 2023.</p>\\n<p>At the these FLL events, teams of students in grades 4 to 8 compete using LEGO robots they design, build, and program to perform complex tasks in a novel challenge created by FIRST every year. The 2023 challenge was called <a href=\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zI_vz9YUw1g\\">Masterpiece</a>, which had an arts theme. (A unified theme carries through each year in all of FIRST's programs, from K-12.) FLL teams also compete by giving presentations they have researched and created on a topic related to the game theme. You can learn more about the 2023 season on the <a href=\\"https://www.first-lego-league.org/en/2023-24-season/the-masterpiece-season\\">FIRST website.</a> The FIRST 2024/25 game is called <a href=\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4JV7x014U0I\\">Submerged</a>, with an ocean theme. FIRST will launch the 2024/25 season on August 6, 2024.</p>\\n<p>The LigerBots host a STEAM expo at the MA East FLL Championship, with hands-on activities for kids in the fields of science, technology, engineering, art, and math, presented by team members and by local organizations. The public is always invited to watch our tournaments and to enjoy the activities at the STEAM expo. More than 1000 people usually attend our two FLL events, including FLL team members, families, LigerBots volunteers, and members of the public.&nbsp;</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>Our STEAM expo at the 2023 MA East FLL Championship included 11 activities provided by local companies and organizations, plus 15 more activities and six robot demonstrations offered by the LigerBots and other FIRST Robotics Competition (FRC) and FIRST Tech Challenge (FTC) teams. If your organization would like to bring a hands-on activity for kids to our next STEAM expo, please email <strong><a href=\\"mailto:fll@ligerbots.org\\">fll@ligerbots.org</a></strong>.</p>\\n<p>You can find hundreds of photos from the 2023 events on the <a href=\\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/ligerbots/albums/\\">LigerBots Flickr</a> page.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">FLL Information Night</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The LigerBots will host its annual FIRST LEGO League (FLL) information night on Saturday, June 22, 2024, from 3:00 to 4:30 p.m., at the<a href=\\"https://www.wpsinstitute.org/wps-cll\\"> WPS Community Learning Lab,</a> 160 Herrick Road, Newton, MA 02459. If you would like to attend &mdash; or cannot attend but are interested &mdash; please fill out <a href=\\"https://forms.gle/sZV4F4t8m43R1Fti8\\">this form.</a></p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>If you cannot attend, a link to the presentation will be posted on this page after the event.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<p>For more background on the FLL program, here is the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll\\">official FLL website</a> and <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/about/vision-and-mission\\">FIRST&rsquo;s mission statement</a>, this year&rsquo;s <a href=\\"https://info.firstinspires.org/first-dive\\">FLL challenge and season info,</a> and the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll/calendar\\">FLL season calendar</a>. You can also email <strong><a href=\\"mailto:fll@ligerbots.org\\">fll@ligerbots.org</a></strong> for more information.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<div class=\\"level4-heading\\">About FIRST<small>&reg;</small> LEGO<small>&reg;</small> League</div>\\n<!-- /wp:tadv/classic-paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>FLL immerses students in grades pre-K to 8 in real-world scientific and technological problems, such as food safety or recycling. There are three levels of FLL based on the students&rsquo; grade: FLL Discover, FLL Explore, and FLL Challenge. FLL Discover is a playful introduction to STEM for children in grades pre-K to 1. FLL Explore, for students in grades 2 to 4, helps students explore while they learn about the design and coding of FLL robots by using powered LEGO&reg; bricks to create solutions to problems. FLL Challenge is a competition for students in grades 4 to 8 that teaches them to build and program autonomous LEGO&reg; robots that can compete on a 4&rsquo; x 8&rsquo; field in the annual robot game designed by the FIRST organization, performing assigned missions such as moving a ball or flipping a switch. FLL Challenge teams also research a real-world problem within guidelines provided by FIRST and design solutions that can be presented to judges at an FLL tournament.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>In addition to helping to teach students about STEAM through hands-on activities, experience in FLL is good preparation for the high-school-level FIRST Robotics Competition (FRC) program, whether with the LigerBots or any other FRC team. The LigerBots are committed to doing what the team can to support FLL programs, including acting as an information clearinghouse and supporting FLL coaches who are figuring out how to get an FLL team up and running.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>New teams are typically formed by two to three parents interested in providing an FLL experience for their kids. Teams typically meet once or twice a week for two to three hours, and the season is approximately 10 weeks long. Parent coaches do not have to be engineers or scientists! The challenge is designed to be solved by kids. Teams can have up to 10 student members. While teams tend to come and go as children enter and then age out of the FLL program, there are a few legacy teams that occasionally have openings for new team members.</p>\\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:tadv/classic-paragraph -->\\n<p>The first requirement for forming a team is to have space for a 4' x 8' game table. It costs about $1100 in materials to start an FLL team and about $500 in recurring costs each year. Startup costs include a robot controller module kit (<a href=\\"https://www.lego.com/en-us/categories/robots-for-kids\\">Spike</a> or EV3), which is about $400, a set of specialized LEGOs called Technics, that costs about another $500, and materials to build a table, which cost about $150. Recurring expenses include a $250 team registration fee that goes to FIRST, a &ldquo;challenge set&rdquo; sold by FIRST for about $100 that includes a game mat and LEGOs specific to that year&rsquo;s challenge, and a registration fee of about $100 per competition for each level the team makes it to. The cost can be divided by the number of participants, or the team can apply for grants. Of course, if teams want to spend more money, they can, but they do not need to. Here is the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll/challenge/pricing-and-payment\\">FLL pricing and payment payment page</a> on the FIRST website.</p>\\n<p>&nbsp;For more information on how to start a team, you can visit the <a href=\\"https://www.firstinspires.org/robotics/fll/start-a-team\\">FIRST&reg; website</a>, or you can email the LigerBots at fll@ligerbots.org.</p>","status":"published"}	\N	\N
